---
/**
 * /process/, and how the work gets made.
 *
 * A prose page, so it takes the site's chrome and then gets out of the way: a
 * display title, display section headings, and body copy at a measure with no
 * motion inside the argument. Only the headings arrive on scroll; every
 * paragraph is painted at full opacity before any ScrollTrigger runs.
 *
 * The copy has been through the ticket 08 editorial pass. No em-dashes: the gate
 * fails the build on one.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageTitle from '../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Chapters from '../components/Chapters.astro';
import { slug } from '../plugins/slug.mjs';

// The chain: each step of the workflow and what that step does. A description
// list rather than a table, because at 375 a two-column table either clips its
// second column inside a scroller a reader has no reason to notice, or crushes
// both columns to two words a line. A dl stacks.
const chain = [
  {
    step: '/grill-me',
    what: 'A design interview. Thirty to eighty questions, one at a time, until the idea stops being vague.',
  },
  { step: '/to-spec', what: 'The interview becomes a written spec.' },
  { step: '/to-tickets', what: 'The spec becomes tracer-bullet tickets with explicit blocking edges.' },
  { step: '/implement', what: 'One ticket: tests first, then the gate, then review, then commit.' },
  { step: 'ralph-once.sh', what: 'The loop, supervised. One ticket at a time, watched.' },
  { step: 'afk-ralph.sh', what: 'The loop, unattended, against the remaining tickets.' },
];

// The three sections of the argument. The h2s below take their ids from the
// same slug the case studies use, so the chapter list and the headings agree.
const sections = ['The chain', 'Four rules, each from a failure', 'The division of labour'];
const chapters = sections.map((title) => ({ title, id: slug(title) }));
const [chainCh, rules, labour] = chapters;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Process"
  description="How the work gets made with agentic coding tools: the plan and execute split, claim files, and the verification gate that makes machine-written code safe to merge."
>
  <PageTitle label="Process" lines={['How I', 'work']} />

  {/* The lede sits in the first screenful and is the whole argument in two
      sentences, so it is deliberately not a [data-reveal]. */}
  <section class="grid gap-8 border-t border-line px-(--gutter) pb-16 pt-8 lg:grid-cols-12">
    <p class="font-display text-3xl font-medium leading-[1.15] text-ink lg:col-span-7">
      I use agentic coding tools heavily. Most of the lines in my recent projects were not typed
      by me.
    </p>
    <p class="measure text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft lg:col-span-4 lg:col-start-9">
      The work is the harness around that: specs, a gate, isolation, and rules about what counts
      as done.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* Same two lanes as a case study: the argument in the first column and the
      chapter list sticky in the second from xl, in flow under it below that. */}
  <div class="xl:grid xl:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_16rem] xl:gap-x-8 xl:pr-(--gutter)">
  <Chapters chapters={chapters} back={{ label: 'All work', href: '/' }} class="xl:sticky xl:top-24 xl:order-last xl:self-start xl:pt-2" />
  <div class="prose pb-24">
    <p>
      The bottleneck stopped being how fast code gets written. It is whether the result can be
      trusted.
    </p>

    <h2 id={chainCh.id} data-reveal>{chainCh.title}</h2>

    <p>
      I adapted this from <a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills">Matt Pocock's skills</a>;
      the skills themselves are in{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/dustinJ15/claude-dotfiles">claude-dotfiles</a>. It's a sequence
      of separate sessions rather than one long conversation:
    </p>

    <dl class="chain">
      {
        chain.map((row, i) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              <span class="chain-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <code>{row.step}</code>
            </dt>
            <dd>{row.what}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <p>
      My adaptation drops the issue tracker. Specs and tickets are markdown under{' '}
      <code>specs/&lt;slug&gt;/</code>, committed alongside the code, so the reasoning arrives in
      the same diff as the change.
    </p>

    <p>
      The interview step carries the most weight. Most bad output traces back to a plan that was
      vague in a way nobody noticed, and eighty questions is a cheap way to find that out.
    </p>

    <h2 id={rules.id} data-reveal>{rules.title}</h2>

    <h3 class="rule">A completion claim is not evidence</h3>
    <p> An agent will report a task as done,
      sincerely, and be wrong. So the rule in my repos is that a claim of completion without the
      command output behind it doesn't count. That's why there's one command that runs the whole
      gate: tests, linting, type checking, security scan. Not because a human couldn't run four
      commands, but because "green" needs a single unambiguous definition that both a person and a
      machine can reach.
    </p>

    <h3 class="rule">Two sessions will collide</h3>
    <p> On one project, two sessions worked the same
      checkout for twenty minutes before either noticed. Plans are now claimed atomically before
      any work starts, with <code>( set -o noclobber; : &gt; &lt;plan&gt;.claim )</code>, which
      either succeeds or fails and cannot do both. Execution happens in a git worktree, so two
      sessions can't share a working tree at all.
    </p>

    <h3 class="rule">Planning and doing should not share a session</h3>
    <p> A session that has been
      writing code is invested in the code it wrote. So planning happens in one session that
      writes only a plan file and touches nothing else, then the context is cleared, and a
      different session executes it. The plan has to be good enough to hand to a stranger, because
      it is being handed to one.
    </p>

    <h3 class="rule">Record what was verified, and how</h3>
    <p> The defect backlog on my
      largest project grades every entry: <strong>CONFIRMED</strong> (a human saw it),{' '}
      <strong>AGENT-VERIFIED</strong> (a machine reproduced it), or <strong>REPORTED</strong>{' '}
      (someone said so). Without that, a plausible-sounding report and a reproduced bug look
      identical three weeks later.
    </p>

    <h2 id={labour.id} data-reveal>{labour.title}</h2>

    <p>
      The agent writes the code. The spec that makes a vague idea buildable, the tests that define
      correct, the gate that makes the answer unambiguous, and the review of every diff before it
      merges are done by hand. When the merge queue stalled for a reason nothing reported, it was
      found by reading the run, not by an agent.
    </p>
  </div>
  </div>

  <section class="border-t border-line px-(--gutter) py-16">
    <p class="label">Read next</p>
    <a
      href="/projects/quote-generator/"
      class="group mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-8 gap-y-2"
    >
      <span
        class="font-display text-[clamp(2rem,9vw,5.5rem)] font-extrabold uppercase leading-none tracking-tight text-ink transition-colors duration-300 group-hover:text-accent"
        >Quote Generator</span
      >
      <span class="label transition-colors group-hover:text-accent">Case study</span
      >
    </a>
    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-6 border-t border-line pt-6">
      <a class="label transition-colors hover:text-accent" href="/">All work</a>
      <a class="label transition-colors hover:text-accent" href="/about/">About</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { withMotion, revealDisplayTitle, revealOnScroll, chapterNav } from '../scripts/motion';

  const nav = document.getElementById('chapters');
  if (nav) chapterNav(nav);

  withMotion(() => {
    revealDisplayTitle();
    // Section headings only. The argument itself is painted before anything
    // scroll-triggered runs, because this page exists to be read.
    revealOnScroll('[data-reveal]');
  });
</script>
